(function(){'use strict';const lib=window.JASPER_FANFIC_DATA;if(!lib||!Array.isArray(lib.series))return;const changes={"stars-between-the-strings":{"su-12":{"number":12,"title":"The Shape of Home","content":"The shape of home changed when I started leaving things in two places.\n\nAt first, it was accidental. A spare shirt ended up in Greg's van after a sudden rainstorm. Then my toothbrush appeared in the small bathroom cabinet because Greg had bought one and said it looked lonely in the package.\n\n“You can take it back,” he said when I noticed it.\n\n“It’s a toothbrush.”\n\n“A toothbrush with choices.”\n\n“You are projecting.”\n\n“I do that with household objects.”\n\nThe toothbrush stayed.\n\nMy scarf remained on the moon-shaped hook. My tea found a permanent place beside Greg's coffee. A stack of my books leaned against the record shelf, and one of my mugs occupied the corner of his kitchenette as though it had always belonged there.\n\nI told myself not to read too much into it.\n\nThen Mara offered me work at the community center.\n\nThe job was part-time, mostly organizing supplies, answering questions, and helping coordinate the evening music sessions. It was not a grand career. It paid enough to keep the bakery from swallowing my entire budget. More importantly, it gave my days a shape that belonged to me.\n\nWhen I told Greg, he lifted me off the ground.\n\n“Put me down,” I said, laughing.\n\n“I’m celebrating.”\n\n“You’re celebrating with my spine.”\n\nHe set I down immediately. “Sorry.”\n\n“You may celebrate with a kiss.”\n\n“That sounds like a reward.”\n\n“It is a mutually beneficial arrangement.”\n\nHe kissed me in the middle of the community center hallway while Mara watched from behind a stack of folding chairs.\n\n“Get a room,” she called.\n\nGreg pulled back. “We have several.”\n\n“That was not an invitation to discuss them.”\n\nI buried my face in Greg's shoulder.\n\nThe weeks that followed were gentle in the way a new routine could be gentle. I worked mornings at the center. Greg played afternoon sets at the car wash or helped Steven with errands. Evenings belonged to shared meals, bad television, and music that spilled through the apartment until the neighbors threatened to complain.\n\nI learned the small geography of his life. Which drawer held the good utensils. Which record made him dance while cooking. How he became quiet when he was worried about Steven. How he sang nonsense lyrics when he could not find the right words.\n\nGreg learned mine. He learned when I needed company and when company meant sitting in another room. He learned which jokes helped and which ones cut too close. He learned that I hated being surprised by touch from behind, and he never forgot.\n\nThe first time he remembered without being reminded, I cried in the grocery store.\n\nGreg stood beside the cart, holding a package of tea. “Did I do something?”\n\n“No.”\n\n“That’s usually not the answer people give while crying near the cereal.”\n\nI wiped my face. “You remembered.”\n\nHe understood. “Oh.”\n\n“You remembered.”\n\n“Of course I did.”\n\nThe words were not dismissive. They were baffled. As if remembering the shape of my comfort was the easiest thing in the world.\n\nI leaned against him. He placed one hand on my shoulder, giving me time to move away.\n\n“I’m going to buy the tea,” I whispered.\n\n“I support this.”\n\n“Then I’m going to buy the expensive cereal.”\n\n“I support this less.”\n\n“You can make up the difference with music.”\n\n“I accept.”\n\nWhen I returned to the apartment, Greg had rearranged the living room. The record shelf stood against the wall. The old chair was gone. In its place was a small couch with enough room for two people and a lamp that cast warm light instead of making the ceiling look haunted.\n\nI stopped in the doorway.\n\n“What is this?”\n\n“A couch.”\n\n“I can see that.”\n\n“I was told I needed to be more precise.”\n\nI walked into the room. The couch was deep and soft. One end held a folded blanket. The other held a pillow in the color I liked.\n\n“You bought a couch.”\n\n“It was on sale.”\n\n“Greg.”\n\n“Mara said the old chair was becoming a safety concern.”\n\n“So you bought a couch.”\n\n“A chair is just a couch with fewer opportunities.”\n\nI touched the armrest. “How much did it cost?”\n\n“Less than the emotional value of the record shelf.”\n\n“That is not an answer.”\n\n“I used money I saved from a gig.”\n\nMy throat tightened. “You didn’t have to do that.”\n\n“I wanted a place where you could sit without judging the furniture.”\n\n“I’m still going to judge it.”\n\n“I know.”\n\nI turned toward him. “Are you trying to make me move in?”\n\nThe question changed the air.\n\nGreg's smile faded. “No.”\n\n“You have my toothbrush. My books. My scarf. Now a couch.”\n\n“I bought a couch because I wanted a couch.”\n\n“For us?”\n\n“For the room.”\n\n“That’s not what I asked.”\n\nGreg stepped closer, then stopped. “I want a life with you. I don’t want to lure you into one with furniture.”\n\nMy chest hurt.\n\n“I’m not saying no,” I said.\n\n“I know.”\n\n“I’m saying I need to know that this is a choice.”\n\n“It is.”\n\n“For both of us.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nThe room was quiet except for the refrigerator. Greg looked nervous now, not charming, not amused. The sincerity made him seem younger and older at once.\n\n“I don’t want to replace your life,” he said. “I don’t want to be the reason you stop having your own place, your own work, your own friends. I want to be the person you come home to because you have chosen me, not because you have nowhere else to go.”\n\nI had no defense against that.\n\n“I want to choose you,” I said.\n\nHe swallowed. “Then we’ll keep making choices.”\n\nI crossed the space between us. “Can I sit on the couch?”\n\n“Please.”\n\nI sat. The cushions gave beneath me. Greg sat beside me, close enough that my thighs touched.\n\n“It’s nice,” I said.\n\n“I’m relieved.”\n\n“It could use a blanket.”\n\nHe pulled the folded one over both my laps.\n\n“Better.”\n\n“I am a man of solutions.”\n\n“You’re a man of blankets.”\n\n“That’s more accurate.”\n\nI leaned into him. Greg wrapped one arm around my shoulders. His mouth brushed my hair.\n\n“Do you want to stay tonight?” he asked.\n\n“I live upstairs.”\n\n“I know.”\n\n“Do you want me to?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Then yes.”\n\nHe kissed me slowly. The couch held us both. The lamp glowed. The new room did not feel like a trap.\n\nIt felt like space being made.\n\nLater, when I woke from a nap with Greg's arm around me, my phone rested on the table beside his. Two lives, still separate, touching at the edges.\n\nI had not moved in.\n\nBut I had stayed.\n\nThat evening, I returned to my own studio and opened every cabinet. The room looked different because I did. The mug on the counter was mine. The scarf in the van was mine. The photograph on Greg's shelf was mine. None of those things required me to surrender the rest of myself.\n\nI called Greg while I folded laundry.\n\n“Are you calling to complain about the couch?” he asked.\n\n“I’m calling to inform you that it has survived another day.”\n\n“A brave piece of furniture.”\n\n“It has also acquired a blanket.”\n\n“Did you leave it there?”\n\n“Maybe.”\n\nHe laughed. “You’re doing it again.”\n\n“I’m allowed to have range.”\n\nThe next box Greg brought upstairs contained a lamp, a packet of old guitar strings and a photograph of him with Steven when Steven was small.\n\n“Uh, I might've overpacked,” he said.\n\n“You brought three things.”\n\n“Emotionally, that's a whole moving truck.”\n\nHe balanced the frame against the windowsill. He hadn't hung it because he wasn't sure whether I wanted it in my apartment. I looked at the boy in the picture, the way Greg stood half bent to match his height, and the awkward little smile caught between them.\n\n“Steven should still have a place in your life when you're here,” I said.\n\n“Yeah. I know. It's just sometimes I worry I'll make this place into the van with better plumbing.”\n\n“I like the van.”\n\n“Don't tell the van. It'll never stop bragging.”\n\nI moved my books aside and made room for the photograph on the shelf. Greg didn't interpret that as an invitation to move everything he owned into my room.\n\n“I want to ask you something,” I said. “When I say I prefer polyamory, that doesn't mean every person I love has access to every place I live. It means the people involved get to negotiate it.”\n\nHe nodded. “Right. Like how I don't show up at Steven's place just because I'm his dad. Except, y'know, more dating and less embarrassing childhood photos.”\n\n“Exactly.”\n\n“So this shelf is your shelf. My picture can visit. My junk drawer does not get automatic citizenship.”\n\n“Your junk drawer needs a passport.”\n\n“Forget a passport. It needs a hazmat team.”\n\nHe laughed, but he asked before he left the lamp. I said yes to the lamp and no to the strings. They went back into his bag, and he didn't treat it as rejection.\n\n\nI folded a shirt and placed it in the drawer. “I’m scared of how easy it is to be with you.”\n\nGreg's voice softened. “Why?”\n\n“Because easy things have never stayed easy.”\n\n“Then we’ll pay attention when they change.”\n\n“You make it sound like maintenance.”\n\n“Love is maintenance. Also music and pastries and occasional dramatic staring.”\n\n“You’re very committed to the pastries.”\n\n“They have never abandoned me.”\n\n“You have been abandoned by a pastry.”\n\n“Once. It was a danish.”\n\nI smiled and sat on the edge of my bed.\n\n“Do you want to come over?” he asked.\n\n“I thought you wanted me to choose.”\n\n“I’m asking, not deciding.”\n\nI looked around my own room. “I’ll come after I finish this.”\n\n“I’ll leave the door open.”\n\n“You have a lock now.”\n\n“I can leave it unlocked.”\n\n“That is not the same thing.”\n\n“I know.”\n\nI finished folding the laundry. Then I walked downstairs, crossed the street, and entered Greg's apartment with my own key.\n\nThe couch was waiting. So was he.\n\nThe ease between us was not proof that nothing would change.\n\nIt was proof that I had learned how to speak when it did.\n\nI stayed until midnight, then walked back upstairs with my own key in my hand and Greg's laughter following me through the stairwell. The studio was still small. The bakery still woke I before dawn. Nothing about the lease had changed.\n\nBut I placed the photograph on my own shelf beside the empty space where the tea belonged, and the room answered with a quiet sense of recognition.\n\nHome, I was learning, did not have to be a single address. It could be a pattern of places where my body stopped preparing to leave.","word_count":1801},"su-25":{"number":25,"title":"The Car Wash in Winter","content":"The car wash sign flickered twice before the pump stopped.\n\nGreg looked up from the change drawer.\n\n\"Oh, come on.\"\n\nOutside, winter rain hammered the asphalt and turned the edges of the parking lot silver. We had exactly one customer waiting, and they were already reconsidering their decision to get a car washed in weather that seemed determined to wash it again for free.\n\nI stepped out beneath the awning.\n\n\"The pump is down,\" I told them. \"We can refund your token or hold it until we're working again.\"\n\nThey asked for the refund.\n\nReasonable.\n\nGreg handed it over, then stared toward the machinery room with the tired expression of a man who had just been informed that his entire day would involve a wrench.\n\n\"We can probably fix it,\" he said.\n\n\"We?\"\n\nHe looked at me.\n\n\"I mean, I can probably fix it.\"\n\n\"Do you know what's wrong?\"\n\n\"Not yet.\"\n\n\"Then maybe we can start with that.\"\n\nHe sighed.\n\nThe car wash was an ordinary little business in Beach City, not a musical stage where charm could fill a missing part. Its winter income paid bills, and the pump had been making an uneven knocking sound for a week. Greg had promised three different people he would take care of it before the next cold spell.\n\nHe hadn't booked a mechanic.\n\nI followed him to the equipment room. He opened the access door, saw a wet patch beneath the pipe, and immediately reached toward the motor.\n\n\"Greg.\"\n\nHe stopped.\n\n\"Power first.\"\n\n\"Right.\"\n\nWe shut the equipment down at the breaker and checked that the indicator had gone dark. I wasn't going to pretend either of us had training to repair a live motor in wet conditions. Greg found the maintenance number on a sticker inside the panel.\n\nHe stared at it.\n\n\"Do you want me to call?\" I asked.\n\n\"I've been telling everybody I can handle this.\"\n\n\"You can handle calling a person who knows how to fix it.\"\n\nHe laughed, but it sounded tired.\n\n\"That's very clever.\"\n\n\"It's also practical.\"\n\nWe called. A technician named Alva could arrive after lunch, weather permitting. She told us not to run the machine again and asked us to photograph the leaking connection from a safe distance. We did.\n\nGreg got the refund envelopes out of the drawer without being asked. There were four prepaid wash tokens on the day's list and a fifth belonging to the customer who had been waiting when the pump stopped.\n\n“I'm gonna call them,” he said.\n\n“You don't have to pretend the machine will be ready tomorrow.”\n\n“Yeah. I was kind of hoping it would save me having to tell them I was wrong about the noise.”\n\nHe picked up the phone and called the first number. He gave the customer the choice of a refund or a visit after the technician cleared the pump, with no guessed reopening time. One person sounded annoyed. Another said they'd been wondering why the water pressure was so uneven last week.\n\nGreg wrote the complaint down. For once he didn't finish a sentence with a joke. The notes might matter to the technician more than his wish to be liked.\n\nWhen he hung up the last call, Steven's name flashed on his screen. Greg stared at it, then answered.\n\n“Hey, kiddo. No, no emergency. Just the car wash deciding that winter is a personal insult.”\n\nI could hear Steven's voice, worried and asking whether he should come help. Greg glanced at the dark equipment room.\n\n“Thanks. I've called somebody who knows the pump. If you wanna stop by later, bring your appetite, not your powers. I'll buy dinner.”\n\nHis shoulders relaxed a little while he listened. He didn't make Steven responsible for getting his father back to being cheerful. He didn't ask me to keep the business afloat out of love. We could be worried with him, and he could still make the responsible call.\n\n\nThe sign went to CLOSED FOR REPAIR.\n\nGreg watched me write it.\n\n\"Think anybody's going to be mad?\"\n\n\"Somebody might. They can still have a refund.\"\n\nHe looked toward the empty wash bay.\n\n\"It's just hard, you know? Everybody thinks I'm the guy who can improvise his way out of anything.\"\n\n\"That sounds more like what you think they think.\"\n\nHe looked at me.\n\n\"You're getting annoying.\"\n\n\"Learned from the best.\"\n\nHe smiled.\n\nWe had time before Alva arrived. I suggested lunch. Greg offered to make sandwiches, then spent ten minutes apologizing for not having the right bread. We ate what he had.\n\nThe rain moved in sheets across the windows.\n\n\"Can I ask something?\" I said.\n\n\"Sure.\"\n\n\"When you told me you needed help, it sounded like you were afraid I'd be disappointed.\"\n\nGreg stared at his sandwich.\n\n\"I like being the fun guy.\"\n\n\"You are the fun guy.\"\n\n\"Yeah, until something breaks. Then I feel like I have to be the guy who fixes it before anybody sees me worry.\"\n\nI rested my elbow on the table.\n\n\"I see you worry.\"\n\n\"Yeah?\"\n\n\"Yes. I like you anyway.\"\n\nHe laughed softly.\n\n\"That's embarrassing.\"\n\n\"Terribly.\"\n\nI reached for his hand. He let me take it.\n\nWe had been careful about our relationship from the beginning: I had told him I was ethically non-monogamous and preferred polyamory, and he had said he wanted to keep choosing us without pretending I could never love anyone else. Practical care was part of that, too. I didn't want him using every repair as proof he deserved my time.\n\nAlva arrived at two. Greg showed her the estimate from the last repair before she opened the pump housing.\n\n\"I don't want to pay twice for the same mistake,\" he said.\n\n\"Neither do I,\" she answered. \"That's why we're testing the actual seal.\"\n\nShe put a small tray beneath the joint and showed us where the water had gathered. The old part had worn unevenly; the replacement needed to match both the pressure and the temperature range. I liked the way she explained the failure without making Greg feel stupid for not knowing it.\n\n\"Can I learn to check that?\" he asked.\n\n\"Yes. But not while the motor's energized.\"\n\nHe stepped back immediately. I grinned. Some lessons didn't need a second request.\n\nAlva brought out a printed checklist showing the safe daily inspection and the signs that meant the machine should be shut down. She didn't give him permission to improvise repairs beyond his training. He thanked her and asked if she could put the next maintenance visit on the calendar while she was already there.\n\nWhen she left to fetch the part, he sat on the office chair.\n\n\"I hate that the place keeps needing money,\" he said.\n\n\"That's what having a business is like.\"\n\n\"Yeah. Doesn't stop it from hurting.\"\n\nI took his hand. He'd spent so much of his life making a van and a guitar do the work of a home; the car wash was ordinary and imperfect in ways he could neither sing away nor romanticize.\n\n\"You can tell me you're scared about the bills,\" I said. \"I'm not here because you have a perfectly functioning pump.\"\n\nHe looked up with an expression that made me want to kiss him.\n\n\"Good, because I definitely don't.\"\n\nI laughed. We made a plan to review the repair account when he wasn't exhausted, and another plan to spend time together that evening if the part arrived safely. Neither promise required either of us to be the only person in the other's life. He'd heard me say I preferred polyamory and ethical non-monogamy; he was learning the equally difficult part of believing I could still want to be with him on a rainy Tuesday.\n\nWhen Alva returned with the correctly rated part, she placed it beside the worn seal and reviewed the estimate again. The motor itself had survived, but the repair bill was still higher than Greg had hoped.\n\nHe looked at the estimate.\n\n\"Could we just patch it?\"\n\n\"Not safely for long,\" Alva said. \"You'd spend more replacing wet components later.\"\n\nGreg asked what the work included. She explained the seal, a pressure test, and a note about the old pipe that might need replacing after winter. He asked when the job could be finished without making her promise a miracle.\n\nWe approved the repair.\n\nI held the umbrella while he paid the deposit.\n\nHe grinned at me.\n\n\"Very heroic.\"\n\n\"The umbrella?\"\n\n\"The not-panicking part.\"\n\n\"That's a work in progress.\"\n\nWhen Alva finished, the pump ran steadily. Greg watched the gauge instead of celebrating immediately. He asked her to show him where the next inspection point was, then wrote the date on the calendar.\n\nThe car wash opened for the final hour of daylight.\n\nTwo customers came. We refunded one old token and sold one new wash. The income did not pay for the repair. Greg recorded the difference instead of pretending the numbers would become friendly if he made enough jokes.\n\nThe last customer had already called to ask when the pump would be safe to use again. Greg looked at the telephone, then at the note Alva had left.\n\n\"Do I tell them tomorrow?\"\n\n\"Tell them what the technician actually tested,\" I said. \"And that we have another inspection on the calendar.\"\n\nHe nodded and made the call.\n\nThe customer asked whether the price would go up. Greg hesitated, then admitted he hadn't decided. He promised to post any change before charging people, rather than making a vague reassurance he might be forced to break.\n\nWhen he hung up, he looked both exhausted and proud.\n\n\"That wasn't fun.\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"But nobody yelled.\"\n\n\"Even if they had, you'd still have been allowed to tell the truth.\"\n\nHe rubbed his eyes.\n\nI took the drawer receipts and offered to tally the final amount while he put the tools away. He said yes instead of insisting he had to do both jobs himself.\n\nWe finished early enough to watch the rain ease over the empty bay.\n\nWe closed on time.\n\nBack at the van, he was quiet.\n\n\"Disappointed?\" I asked.\n\n\"In the pump? Absolutely.\"\n\n\"In me?\"\n\nHe looked startled.\n\n\"Never.\"\n\nI smiled.\n\n\"Then perhaps you can extend me the same courtesy when I can't fix something.\"\n\nHe leaned toward me.\n\n\"That, I think I can do.\"\n\nI kissed him, slow and warm, smelling rain on his jacket. He laughed against my mouth when the van's windshield wipers switched on by accident.\n\n\"Romantic soundtrack,\" he said.\n\n\"Very expensive.\"\n\nWe changed out of our wet clothes later and kept the rest of the evening private. Before bed Greg put the technician's card in the drawer beside his guitar picks.\n\nThe next morning the pump held pressure.\n\nThe sign stayed on.\n\nNo fireworks, no magical rescue, just a repaired machine and a person who had learned that asking for help did not make his love smaller.","word_count":1815},"su-47":{"number":47,"title":"The Night After the Show","content":"The last cable had tangled itself around Greg's ankle.\n\nThat was the only explanation I could find for why a man who had spent decades handling concert equipment was standing in the middle of Starwake Pier looking personally betrayed by a length of black cord.\n\n\"I swear it wasn't like that when I put it down,\" he said.\n\nI looked at the knot.\n\n\"Maybe the cable has a private life.\"\n\n\"Very funny.\"\n\n\"It could be ethically non-monogamous. Perhaps it's seeing the speaker cable and the microphone stand.\"\n\nHe laughed so hard he had to lean against the equipment case.\n\n\"Don't tell me you're jealous of a cable.\"\n\n\"Only of how much attention you're giving it.\"\n\nHe grinned and reached for my hand, but the cable tightened around his boot.\n\n\"Hold that thought.\"\n\nI bent down and unwound the loop.\n\nThe Listening Fair had ended an hour earlier. The crowd was gone, the tea table had been cleaned, and the donation box had been counted with Mara present. The temporary lights were off except for the safety lanterns along the accessible path.\n\nWe had agreed to finish the cleanup before leaving.\n\nGreg had agreed very enthusiastically.\n\nNow he was trying to make an enemy out of electrical equipment.\n\n\"Maybe you should write a song about it,\" I said.\n\n\"I already have three verses.\"\n\n\"Of course you do.\"\n\nHe hummed something ridiculous as we packed the cables. I joined in with a deliberately awful harmony. His smile grew until he couldn't keep the melody straight.\n\nIt was the kind of evening I liked best with him. No performance required, no audience expecting us to turn love into a single perfect story. Just the two of us, a lot of equipment, and the small satisfaction of work actually finished.\n\nWe carried the last case to the storage shed.\n\nI checked the lock.\n\nGreg checked it again.\n\n\"You're learning from Danse,\" I said.\n\n\"Hey, his system works.\"\n\n\"Only after we removed the part that needed a ladder.\"\n\nGreg laughed.\n\nWe started toward the Starwake Door.\n\nThe night air was cool enough that I pulled my coat tighter. He noticed without making a fuss and offered me the spare scarf he'd brought. It was faded blue, soft from years of use, and smelled faintly of laundry soap.\n\n\"Thanks,\" I said.\n\n\"Always.\"\n\nHe slipped an arm around my shoulders as we walked.\n\nFor a while we didn't talk. The sound of the sea moved beneath the pier. Lanterns glowed along the path back to Lanternwell Square, where a few late visitors were making their way home from the other districts.\n\nThen Greg stopped.\n\n\"Can I ask you something?\"\n\n\"Of course.\"\n\n\"Are you happy?\"\n\nThe question was so simple that I didn't know how to answer it.\n\n\"Right now?\"\n\n\"Yeah. Right now.\"\n\nI looked at him.\n\n\"I'm tired. My feet hurt. I have a notebook full of repairs we still need to schedule. And I'm happy.\"\n\nHe smiled.\n\n\"Good.\"\n\n\"Are you?\"\n\n\"Yeah.\"\n\nHe looked toward the dark water.\n\n\"I used to think happy meant getting to the part after everything difficult. The concert's over, the money's enough, everybody's okay, nobody's upset.\"\n\n\"That sounds exhausting.\"\n\n\"It was.\"\n\nHe rubbed his thumb over my knuckles.\n\n\"Now I think it can just be this. A night where we did something good and still have a few problems to deal with tomorrow.\"\n\nI leaned closer.\n\n\"That's a very nice way to say we have chores.\"\n\n\"Romantic, right?\"\n\n\"Unbelievably.\"\n\nHe kissed my cheek.\n\nI turned toward him.\n\n\"Was that all you had planned?\"\n\nHe raised an eyebrow.\n\n\"I could do better.\"\n\n\"Could you?\"\n\n\"Challenge accepted.\"\n\nHe kissed me properly this time, warm and slow, the kind that made the cold air feel less important. His hand rested lightly at my waist. I held the edge of his jacket and smiled against his mouth.\n\nWhen we parted, he looked a little breathless.\n\n\"Better?\"\n\n\"Much.\"\n\nHe grinned.\n\nNola caught us before we left. Her sleeves were pushed to her elbows, and she'd been carrying the same clipboard since the first soundcheck.\n\n\"Greg, you're done,\" she said.\n\n\"I am aware.\"\n\n\"Then stop taking cables out of other people's hands.\"\n\nHe blinked at the coil he'd just reached for. \"I thought I was helping.\"\n\n\"You are. But Lio's crew is paid for cleanup and you have an actual life to go home to.\"\n\nGreg looked at me, his hair falling into his eyes. \"Do I?\"\n\n\"Allegedly.\"\n\nNola checked a box on the sheet. \"Also, your guitar case is in the wrong storage bay.\"\n\n\"You're destroying my reputation.\"\n\n\"The case weighs more than the small instrument shelf can safely hold.\"\n\nHe took it without arguing. I loved that his first response was a joke and his second was to fix the problem. Not every lesson needed a solemn speech.\n\nOutside, we stood under the covered path while a drizzle began to spot the lantern glass. Greg watched the crew finish rolling the last cable without him. He looked oddly lonely for a moment.\n\n\"I always used to stay until the last person left,\" he said. \"Then I could tell myself I'd been necessary.\"\n\n\"Did it work?\"\n\n\"Sometimes. Mostly it meant I was too tired to enjoy the ride home.\"\n\nHe tucked his guitar safely under the awning. \"You know I'm still gonna worry when you travel to see the others.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"But you've told me you're ethically non-monogamous and you prefer polyamory. I don't want to make you prove you care by missing your own plans.\"\n\nI caught the front of his jacket, tugging him close enough for our foreheads to touch.\n\n\"You can tell me you miss me,\" I said. \"I don't mind. I just don't want missing me to become a verdict.\"\n\nHe smiled. \"That's the least catchy song title I ever heard.\"\n\n\"It'll be a hit.\"\n\nHe hummed a deliberately dreadful chorus under his breath. I laughed, and he kissed the corner of my mouth before asking whether I wanted to go back to Beach City. I did. Nola's clipboard remained at Starwake, in the hands of somebody who knew exactly where the cables belonged.\n\nWe went through the Starwake Door into Beach City, where the van waited beneath a streetlamp. The familiar smell of the coast met me: salt, asphalt, and somebody's late-night food from a nearby shop.\n\nGreg opened the van and set down his guitar.\n\n\"Want to stay?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nHe looked at me for another moment.\n\n\"Because you want to?\"\n\n\"Because I want to.\"\n\nHe smiled, relief and affection moving together across his face.\n\nInside, we folded the blanket, put the equipment list on the dashboard, and made a small space where there had been too many things. Greg found a packet of crackers and offered them with great ceremony.\n\n\"A feast.\"\n\n\"Truly, you spoil me.\"\n\n\"I try.\"\n\nWe ate the crackers and laughed about the fair. He imitated Toph declaring the practice platform \"almost competent.\" I tried to imitate Iroh's calm reply and failed because I started laughing halfway through the sentence.\n\nThen the laughter settled.\n\nGreg reached for my hand.\n\n\"I know you have other people you love,\" he said quietly. \"I know we talked about ethical non-monogamy and polyamory, and I know it doesn't make tonight less ours.\"\n\nI squeezed his fingers.\n\n\"It doesn't.\"\n\n\"I just wanted to say that I like having nights that belong to us because we choose them.\"\n\n\"Me too.\"\n\nHe leaned close.\n\nThe next kiss was unhurried. There was no need to fill the silence with a joke, though I knew he would if I asked. I rested my forehead against his and listened to the small sounds of the van cooling in the night.\n\n\"Stay with me,\" he whispered.\n\n\"I am.\"\n\nWe turned down the light and let the rest of the evening be private.\n\nIn the morning, sunlight came through the windshield and woke me before the alarm. Greg was still asleep, one hand resting near the folded equipment list. His hair looked as though it had been involved in a disagreement with a pillow.\n\nI smiled.\n\nI was tempted to let him sleep.\n\nThen I remembered that the repair team was expecting the donation total by nine.\n\nI nudged his shoulder.\n\n\"Greg.\"\n\nA muffled sound.\n\n\"Greg, we have responsibilities.\"\n\n\"Tell them I'm a guitar.\"\n\n\"That makes no sense.\"\n\n\"Great guitars don't do paperwork.\"\n\nI laughed.\n\nHe opened one eye.\n\n\"Coffee?\"\n\n\"After the totals are sent.\"\n\n\"Brutal.\"\n\nWe made coffee anyway, then sat together at the small table outside the van and checked the ledger. The donations covered the new fasteners and some of the lumber, exactly as we'd announced. We sent the figures to Tamsin and copied the volunteers who had helped. No exaggeration. No promise that the rest of the repairs would happen by magic.\n\nGreg looked at the final number.\n\n\"Not everything we wanted.\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Enough for the next step.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nHe leaned over and kissed my temple.\n\n\"Good morning.\"\n\n\"Good morning.\"\n\nGreg checked the payment ledger a second time before we left the van. One of the lines for the sound technician was marked *volunteer honorarium*, which sounded suspiciously like money somebody might decide not to pay.\n\n“Hold up,” he said. “The technician was hired to check the power. That's not an honorarium.”\n\nThe person at the desk said the fundraiser had been short on cash and that perhaps she would accept a credit on the program.\n\nGreg stared at the paper. “I love a good credit. Can't exactly put it in the gas tank, though.”\n\nHe asked for the original work order. It listed a fixed inspection fee, agreed before the event. The technician had already done the work and passed the stage on the condition that the unsafe circuit remain closed.\n\n“We owe the fee,” Greg said. “It's not a favor we can turn into applause.”\n\nThere was some uncomfortable arithmetic. Nola checked which donations had been restricted to the rail repairs, then called the committee member who had approved the technician's booking. They agreed to cover the invoice from the event operating account and carry the lower railing balance forward honestly.\n\nGreg looked tired when it was done, the kind of tired no encore would cure.\n\n“You okay?” I asked.\n\n“Yeah. Just realizing a musician isn't a charity because somebody printed 'benefit' on the poster.”\n\n“You've done a lot.”\n\n“So have you. And you're allowed to go home before the chairs.”\n\nI pressed my forehead against his shoulder. He hummed the first four notes of our unfinished song, badly out of tune, and I laughed into his jacket.\n\nWe still had people to thank and a real repair to finish. We didn't need to make anybody's unpaid labor the price of the good night we'd had.\n\n\nBy the time we returned to Starwake Pier, the volunteers were already moving supplies to the safe storage area. Toph had left a note about checking the platform after the next rain. Iroh had sent a small tin of the new house tea blend as thanks.\n\nWe got to work.\n\nThat was the best part of the night after the show: the morning wasn't a punishment for being happy.\n\nIt was simply the next part of our life.","word_count":1871}},"the-shore-i-choose":{"maui-12":{"number":12,"title":"A Place for the Departing","content":"The first person to ask whether they were allowed to leave the Lanternweald without saying goodbye was standing in my kitchen.\n\nThey were holding a loaf of bread in both hands. Mara had baked it that morning, and the crust had split along the top like a small mountain range. The traveler had bought it with two copper buttons and an offer to mend the bakery curtains.\n\n\"I can give the bread back,\" they said.\n\n\"Why?\"\n\n\"If it makes things easier.\"\n\nI stared at them. They stared at the floor.\n\nIt was the third time they had come to see me that week, and the first time they'd been able to ask the question.\n\n\"You can leave,\" I said. \"You don't owe anyone a reason.\"\n\nThe traveler's shoulders loosened. Not all the way.\n\n\"What about the register?\"\n\n\"Neri's arrival book?\"\n\nThey nodded.\n\n\"It's not supposed to be a leash.\"\n\n\"Everyone knows when I come in. Pip reads out the visitor names sometimes. I don't want anyone knowing which road I'm taking.\"\n\nI had assumed that a place full of doors would make leaving simple. I'd assumed many things since arriving here. My notebook was becoming a monument to the hazards of assuming.\n\n\"Can you wait until I check how the departure book works?\" I asked.\n\nThey hesitated.\n\n\"You don't have to wait,\" I corrected. \"I can meet you at the door later, if you want help. Or you can go now.\"\n\n\"Later,\" they said. \"And please don't tell the man with the hook.\"\n\nI found the public route map beneath a stack of bakery receipts. The safe path had Edda's turning measurements and Fen's tide window drawn in two colors. Nowhere did it require a traveler's name.\n\n\"Do you want this?\" I asked.\n\n\"Only if you don't tell anyone which door I'm taking.\"\n\nMara knocked on the open kitchen door. She had come to ask whether the traveler's offer to repair the curtains still stood. They froze.\n\n\"Only if you want the work,\" Mara told them. \"The bread was paid for. I can hire someone else to sew the curtains.\"\n\n\"I can't stay until tomorrow.\"\n\n\"Then I'll ask someone else.\"\n\nNo complaint, no story about gratitude. Mara returned to her ovens. The curtains still needed mending; that didn't make the traveler responsible for them.\n\n\"I don't want to leave as somebody else's story,\" they whispered.\n\n\"You don't owe anybody the ending.\"\n\nI'd started writing their initial beside the route. I tore off the little corner and felt embarrassed by how automatic the motion had been. Keeping a person safe should not mean keeping them under observation.\n\nOutside, someone dragged something heavy across the cobbles. Maui's voice boomed about another heroic harbor achievement.\n\nThe traveler looked up, panicked.\n\nI folded the map with the directions facing inward and set it beside the bread. \"Take this when you're ready. I'll close the window.\"\n\nMaui chose that precise moment to come through the window.\n\nNot the door. The window.\n\nHe had a string of damp rope over one shoulder and sand in his hair. One foot landed on the kitchen stool. The stool protested so loudly that the traveler flinched.\n\n\"Good news!\" Maui announced. \"I secured the—\"\n\n\"Window,\" I said.\n\n\"Very efficient route.\"\n\n\"Door.\"\n\n\"Slower route.\"\n\nThe traveler went white.\n\nI set the bread on the table and stepped between them and the window.\n\n\"Maui, I need you outside for a minute.\"\n\nHe looked at my face and lost the grin.\n\n\"Of course.\"\n\nThat was progress. Last month he would have asked five questions and answered three of them himself.\n\nAfter he climbed back out, I walked the traveler to the covered passage behind the bakery. They gave me the name they'd chosen for the arrival book: Lark. They said it softly, like a name they weren't certain they were permitted to keep.\n\nWe agreed on a meeting place near the eastern arch at dusk. They did not tell me why they were leaving, and I did not ask.\n\nWhen I returned, Maui was standing outside with the injured expression of a man denied the chance to make a heroic speech.\n\nHe kicked at a pebble with enough force to send it across the courtyard.\n\n\"Great. I made a stranger afraid of me without even getting to do anything heroic. New personal record.\"\n\n\"Not every stranger needs the hero,\" I said.\n\n\"Some don't even want to meet him.\" Maui looked genuinely offended by that possibility, then caught my expression. \"Okay, fine. Most people do. A few have terrible taste.\"\n\nMini Maui sat on his chest, made an exaggerated sad face, then kicked an imaginary door shut. Maui covered the tattoo before it could complete the pantomime.\n\n\"I wasn't going to follow Lark,\" he said. \"I just wanted to know whether there was a reason they were scared.\"\n\n\"That's their story.\"\n\n\"I know the rule.\" Maui picked at the seam of his hook strap. \"I've wrestled monsters and argued with the sun. Apparently my newest weakness is not being everyone's favorite person.\" He gave me a sideways grin that didn't quite last. \"Terrible development for my reputation.\"\n\nThe honesty lasted a heartbeat. Then he straightened to his full height and announced that he was going to find something very heavy to lift while he contemplated his many admirable qualities. He chose a sack of flour Mara had already asked someone to move. That was about as healthy a response as I expected from him that day.\n\n\"Did I scare someone?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"I didn't know they were there.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\nHe folded his arms. \"If someone needs to leave, I can escort them. I know every safe path from the cove.\"\n\n\"They explicitly asked me not to tell you.\"\n\nHis expression sharpened.\n\n\"Do they think I would hurt them?\"\n\n\"I don't know.\"\n\n\"Then why—\"\n\n\"Because that's their choice.\"\n\nHe looked toward the bakery. A child was chasing a dropped apple across the square. The world went on around his silence.\n\n\"You think I can't be trusted,\" he said.\n\n\"I think you want to be useful. That's not the same thing as being invited.\"\n\nMaui turned away.\n\nI let him have the space.\n\nBy afternoon I had a list of problems. Some were obvious: Pip sometimes announced arrival names before asking permission. The arrival book had a column labeled EXPECTED RETURN, as though every departure were a promise. Neri kept a spare key for every door but hadn't written down who could request one. The eastern arch had a railing that blocked one side of the approach.\n\nThe least obvious problem was that everyone wanted to help in their own preferred way. Which meant that a traveler who needed privacy had to reject kindness five times before getting what they'd asked for.\n\nNeri listened while I explained.\n\nThey rested both hands over their walking stick.\n\n\"The register was meant to protect travelers. If someone went missing, we wanted to know which way to search.\"\n\n\"Search only if they've asked to be searched for,\" I said.\n\nNeri's eyes dimmed. \"That is a difficult thing to say after you've lost someone.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"Do you?\"\n\nI thought of the door to my old life. All the people I couldn't name, some of whom might be looking for me. I thought of how sick I felt when someone assumed not remembering meant I belonged to whoever recognized me first.\n\n\"Yes,\" I said. \"More than I'd like.\"\n\nNeri looked toward the arches.\n\n\"We used to close them in storms.\"\n\n\"Did the traveler agree?\"\n\nThey did not answer.\n\nThe silence bothered me enough to write it down.\n\nBefore dusk I took the blank register to Pip, who maintained the crossing notices. They looked at the page and then at me.\n\n“People used to be grateful we could tell their families where they'd gone,” Pip said.\n\n“Some of them were. Lark isn't.”\n\nPip tapped the line marked EXPECTED RETURN. “If I leave this blank, what happens when somebody asks me whether they should be worried?”\n\n“We ask whether the traveler requested help. If not, we say we can't disclose their route.”\n\nPip didn't look satisfied. Neri came over with a bundle of old forms and listened as we tried three versions of the wording. Each sounded either too harsh or like a promise that someone would be watched whether they wanted it or not.\n\nMaui arrived carrying a barrel on one shoulder, the heavy thing he'd evidently found to lift while thinking. He put it down very carefully.\n\n“You need a sign?” he asked. “I can paint one. The great Maui's official departure advice. Very tasteful. Huge letters.”\n\n“What if the sign said somebody can travel without asking permission?” I asked.\n\nHe looked at the register. “Then the big letters can say that.”\n\n“And no names.”\n\nMini Maui made an extravagant show of holding a finger to its lips. Maui grinned, then looked at Lark's blank row.\n\n“I'm not telling anyone which door they use,” he said. “Even if they ask nicely.”\n\nPip took the form back and drew a line through the preprinted request for a future return date. The actual ledger change would need approval in the morning. Tonight, there would be no announcement and no crowd at the arch.\n\n\nAt dusk I met Lark beside the eastern arch. The air smelled of wet stone and burned rosemary. They had one travel pack and the loaf of bread wrapped in cloth.\n\n\"Are you coming with me?\" they asked.\n\n\"Only as far as you want.\"\n\n\"Can you stand back while I open it?\"\n\n\"Of course.\"\n\nI went to the railing and stood facing the square. Behind me a brass latch clicked. Cold air touched my neck.\n\n\"Jasper?\"\n\nI turned.\n\nLark was waiting in the doorway, their shoulders straight now.\n\n\"Thank you for not asking.\"\n\n\"Safe travels.\"\n\nThey smiled once and stepped through. The door closed.\n\nNothing terrible happened. No bell rang in protest. The lights at the well stayed lit.\n\nI found Maui at the cove checking the new tide chart. He had covered the little crab drawing with a square of parchment, then uncovered it again, as if even the crab's expression needed approval.\n\n\"They left,\" I said.\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"How?\"\n\n\"I saw the door light change.\" He paused. \"I stayed here.\"\n\nI leaned against the railing beside him.\n\n\"Was it hard?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nI appreciated the answer.\n\nHe stared at the horizon. \"I've spent so much of my life proving I can get someone home. I never considered that getting out of the way might be the useful thing.\"\n\nI found his hand where it rested on the rail. He turned his palm up.\n\n\"I didn't tell you because Lark asked me not to,\" I said. \"Not because I don't trust you. Sometimes someone's privacy belongs to them even when keeping it makes us uncomfortable.\"\n\n\"I know that now.\" He glanced sideways. \"I might forget it.\"\n\n\"Then I'll remind you.\"\n\n\"You're very good at being annoying.\"\n\n\"I've been practicing.\"\n\nHe lifted my hand and kissed my knuckles. The gesture was so unexpected that warmth climbed up my neck.\n\n\"May I?\" he asked, close enough that his voice lowered.\n\n\"You may.\"\n\nHe kissed me gently beside the new tide sign. The sea moved beneath us. He did not promise to be perfect, and I did not need him to.\n\nThe following morning, Pip and I rewrote the arrival register. There would be an optional contact and a private note indicating whether someone wanted assistance leaving. Nobody would read names aloud without permission. And where the old page said EXPECTED RETURN, we wrote one question instead:\n\nDO YOU WANT SOMEONE TO WAIT FOR YOU?\n\nLark's row remained blank.\n\nFor the first time, I understood that a blank line could be an act of care.","word_count":1962},"maui-25":{"number":25,"title":"Mereana Returns","content":"Mereana's boat arrived without a banner.\n\nThat was the first thing I noticed. The second was that she stepped onto the dock carrying a toolbox, not an instrument.\n\nMaui had been expecting a performance. He'd told three different people that the visiting artists from Aotearoa might be bringing a celebration to the cove, and by noon half the village was hovering near the landing.\n\nMereana looked at the crowd and raised one eyebrow.\n\n\"Did someone announce a festival?\"\n\nMaui looked at me. The grin he normally wore when a crowd gathered had frozen halfway across his face. \"Technically,\" he muttered, \"I may have advertised a magnificent artistic occasion before asking the artists whether they wanted one. A minor oversight. Possibly a large oversight.\"\n\nI looked at Maui.\n\n\"Apparently,\" I said, \"someone did.\"\n\nHer group had come to inspect a set of carved panels they had lent to the local heritage workshop. They were returning after an exhibition elsewhere, and Mereana had scheduled a short visit because she wanted to see how the panels were being stored and whether the agreement still suited her group.\n\nIt was their work. They could choose when to display it, discuss it, or take it home.\n\nPip hurried down the steps carrying a clipboard.\n\n\"We prepared the hall,\" they said. \"We can set up chairs if you'd like to perform.\"\n\n\"That's kind,\" Mereana answered. \"But we didn't come to perform.\"\n\nThe disappointment moved through the crowd like a breeze.\n\nA visitor standing near the rope store spoke before I could.\n\n\"But aren't you here to do the traditional courtship dance? Someone told me your group teaches the old way people choose partners.\"\n\nMereana went still.\n\n\"No,\" she said.\n\nThe visitor blinked.\n\n\"I read somewhere—\"\n\n\"Where?\"\n\n\"I don't remember.\"\n\n\"Then perhaps don't explain my people to me from a thing you can't remember.\"\n\nHer voice wasn't raised. She didn't need it to be.\n\nI felt my face grow hot on her behalf, which was a familiar and unhelpful sensation. I wanted to step in, make a speech about respect, and rescue everyone from the silence.\n\nMereana was perfectly capable of responding herself.\n\nI stayed beside the supply cart.\n\nThe visitor looked uncomfortable.\n\n\"I didn't mean offense.\"\n\n\"Then you can ask about what we're actually doing,\" Mereana said. \"We are artists. Some of our work draws on specific traditions with specific histories. Those aren't interchangeable with a universal romance ritual, and they aren't yours to assign us because you've come expecting entertainment.\"\n\nPip lowered the clipboard.\n\n\"Would you prefer we close the hall to the public?\"\n\n\"Not necessarily. I'd like to inspect the panels. Then we'll decide whether there's anything we want to share.\"\n\nMaui stepped forward.\n\nMini Maui popped up on his shoulder and raised both arms for the applause that wasn't coming. Maui gave him a long, injured stare.\n\n\"I may have been a tiny bit enthusiastic,\" he admitted.\n\nMereana folded her arms. \"You told half the cove we were doing a performance.\"\n\n\"That sounds worse when you say it.\"\n\n\"It should.\"\n\nHe glanced at me as if I might help him charm his way free. I raised my eyebrows. He sighed with the sort of theatrical suffering normally reserved for a cursed demigod.\n\n\"Okay. Here's what happened. I got excited, pictured a brilliant show, and forgot to ask the people who'd actually have to do it. Big mistake. Very on-brand mistake. Still mine.\"\n\nMereana studied him for a moment. \"We came to inspect the lending rack, Maui. The work is ours to plan.\"\n\n\"Then the famous Maui will provide only the services requested.\" He drew himself upright. \"Heavy objects moved. Doors held. Extraordinary smiling.\"\n\n\"Start with the doors,\" she said.\n\nHe bowed extravagantly. The apology was real, but so was his need to make somebody smile. Mereana didn't owe him a performance for trying.\n\n\"I told people you might perform. I shouldn't have.\"\n\nMereana looked at him.\n\n\"No, you shouldn't.\"\n\nHe nodded.\n\n\"I'll correct it.\"\n\n\"Good.\"\n\nThat could have been the end of the matter, except the panels had been moved.\n\nNot stolen. Not damaged deliberately. Someone had relocated them to a storage room where the air was drier, without telling Mereana's group. The new room was locked, and the person with the key was away at the market.\n\nMereana looked at the empty display rack.\n\n\"That wasn't our agreement.\"\n\nNeri arrived with the lending record and apologized. There was no excuse to invent. The workshop had made a decision about someone else's work without consulting its owners.\n\nWe found the keyholder. We inspected the storage room. Two panels were fine, one had warped slightly against a support that put pressure on its edge, and the fourth had been wrapped in fabric that trapped moisture.\n\nMereana asked the steward to bring the original loan agreement. The sheet had a list of objects and an expected return date, but nothing about humidity, handling, or who could decide whether the panels were displayed.\n\n“This isn't a storage contract,” she said. “It's a list of things you took.”\n\nThe steward looked embarrassed. “We wanted visitors to see the work.”\n\n“Then you should have asked the people who made it what they were willing to show.”\n\nMaui shifted his weight. I recognized the dangerous beginning of a speech about art bringing people together. He caught my eye and seemed to reconsider. Mini Maui had both tiny hands over its mouth, which was probably the closest it would ever come to looking helpful.\n\nThe visiting artist who had brought the measuring tools explained which panel could be handled safely and which had to remain untouched until it dried. Mereana wrote those instructions down in their own words. She also asked for the lending form to identify who had authority to approve a repair rather than assuming any helpful stranger could make one.\n\n“Would it be better to leave the whole collection here?” I asked.\n\n“Better for whom?” she replied.\n\nI looked at the warped edge. The answer could not be the workshop's enthusiasm, or Maui's pride, or my hope that the problem would vanish with an apology.\n\n“Better for the owners,” I said.\n\n“Then ask them,” she answered.\n\nWe sent a note with the damage description and the proposed packing method. Mereana retained the right to reject both the repair and the delay. The steward wrote down the cost of another crossing instead of promising that the artists would somehow fit it into their schedule.\n\n\nMereana took notes.\n\n\"Will it recover?\" I asked.\n\n\"Possibly. We need to examine it properly.\"\n\nThe workshop steward offered to pay for a repair.\n\nMereana nodded.\n\n\"That's part of it. The other part is changing the handling process.\"\n\nOne of the visiting artists unfolded a travel schedule. Their next paid engagement was already booked. Because the panels had been stored incorrectly, they would miss a boat connection and have to arrange another.\n\nPip stared at the numbers. \"I didn't realize you had another booking.\"\n\n\"We didn't come here to be available indefinitely,\" Mereana said. \"The workshop needs to discuss the extra costs with its owners. Not ask our performers to absorb them.\"\n\nMaui planted a hand on the doorframe. \"I could carry the whole collection to the next port! Faster than any boat! Unbelievably handsome courier, too.\"\n\nThe artist looked at the warped panel. \"Can you guarantee it won't take any more strain?\"\n\n\"These hands have hauled islands out of the ocean.\"\n\n\"That wasn't the question,\" I said.\n\nHe gave me a wounded look. Then, to Mereana: \"Fine. Who's moving it? And how?\"\n\n\"Nobody moves anything until we agree on the supports.\"\n\nMaui stepped aside so the artist carrying the measuring tools could pass. Pip crossed the imaginary performance off the public notice and started a separate expense sheet, rather than putting out a jar marked *entertainment*.\n\n\"My great heroic destiny today,\" Maui muttered to me, \"is to keep a doorway clear.\"\n\n\"Want a medal?\"\n\n\"Naturally. But lunch will do.\"\n\nMereana heard him and snorted. Her group still didn't owe the village a performance. The expenses, the panel and the next decision remained theirs.\n\nShe asked for a revised lending form that named who could move the work, under what conditions, and how the owners would be contacted. Neri agreed to help draft it. Tamsin volunteered to make a better storage rack, but only after Mereana approved the design.\n\nMaui carried the old rack outside.\n\nHe didn't make a speech.\n\nI found him by the door, watching the group examine the panels.\n\n\"Still wish it were a festival?\" I asked.\n\n\"A little.\"\n\n\"Me too.\"\n\nHe looked surprised.\n\n\"I like hearing people share beautiful things. I just keep forgetting that choosing not to share is part of the beauty too.\"\n\nI nodded.\n\nMereana accepted the tea, then asked whether the chairs could be moved to somewhere the wind wouldn't blow dust into the cups. We carried them into the workshop's small courtyard, where a canvas awning had been pulled taut between two posts.\n\n\"I was worried you'd leave angry,\" Maui admitted.\n\n\"I was angry,\" she answered. \"I also wanted the panels checked. Both things can be true.\"\n\nHe looked at her over the rim of his cup.\n\n\"The work means something to the people who made it,\" she continued. \"Some of them are here, some couldn't travel. I was entrusted to speak about the loan. That doesn't make me the owner of everyone's story.\"\n\nNeri returned with a draft form. Mereana read the first page and pointed out that it included permission to photograph the panels for publicity. Her group had agreed to conservation photographs, not promotional images.\n\nNeri crossed out the sentence and wrote two separate options.\n\nMaui watched. \"I thought the village would be honored to display them.\"\n\n\"It was,\" Mereana said. \"But honor isn't the same as permission.\"\n\nWhen she excused herself to speak to the other artists, he slumped in his chair.\n\n\"I wanted them to feel celebrated.\"\n\n\"I know. Next time ask what celebration they want.\"\n\nHe laughed without much humor. \"Your next sentence is going to be that I don't get to choose the music.\"\n\n\"You can choose what music you play yourself.\"\n\nHe considered that, then drew a small circle on the table with his fingertip.\n\n\"Will you still dance with me after I embarrass myself?\"\n\n\"If you ask me and I want to.\"\n\nHe looked up, relief plain on his face. \"I will ask.\"\n\nWe helped carry the repaired rack back inside after Mereana approved its position. She checked the supports herself. The next morning's signed form would matter more than anything Maui could have announced from a stage, but the repaired storage space was a kind of welcome too.\n\nA little later Mereana joined us outside. She seemed tired, but not angry enough to leave immediately.\n\n\"Would you like tea?\" I asked.\n\n\"Yes. And a chair.\"\n\nI brought both.\n\nShe sat beneath the awning and watched the harbor.\n\n\"I don't mind people being curious,\" she said. \"I mind when curiosity arrives with a costume already picked out for me.\"\n\nMaui looked at his hands.\n\n\"I've done that to people.\"\n\n\"We all can,\" she said. \"The question is whether we notice, and what we do afterward.\"\n\nI thought of my own missing memories, the way strangers sometimes decided who I must have been from a few fragments. I understood the shape of her frustration without pretending the histories were the same.\n\nMereana turned toward me.\n\n\"How is the new garden?\"\n\n\"Small. Salty. Stubborn.\"\n\n\"Sounds like a place worth seeing.\"\n\nWe walked there together after the storage inspection. Fen explained the beds and how visitors could contribute without importing plants that damaged the dunes. Mereana was delighted by the little flowers growing untouched beside the signal post.\n\n\"Those were here before the beds?\" she asked.\n\n\"Yes,\" Fen said.\n\n\"Good. I like a place that leaves room for what was already living.\"\n\nBy sunset, the visiting group had decided to stay one more day to work on the storage agreement. They did not perform. Nobody pressured them.\n\nMaui cooked supper. Mereana told us about a boat repair she had supervised back home, mostly to complain about how much easier it was to give advice than to keep a project on budget. Maui confessed he was learning the same thing.\n\nShe laughed.\n\nWhen the meal ended, she stood and stretched.\n\n\"Thank you for asking what we wanted,\" she said.\n\nI looked at the untouched instruments beside her bag.\n\n\"Thank you for trusting us enough to tell us no.\"\n\nAfter she left, Maui and I washed the cups.\n\n\"Was that a successful visit?\" he asked.\n\n\"She got her work inspected, the process changed, and she chose to stay. I'd say yes.\"\n\n\"No performance.\"\n\n\"No performance.\"\n\nHe turned toward me.\n\n\"Can I sing?\"\n\nI stared at him.\n\n\"No.\"\n\nHe laughed.\n\nI kissed him before he could begin anyway.\n\nThe following morning, the lending form had new boxes for consent to move, display, repair, and return the panels. None were checked by default.\n\nMereana read it, made three corrections, and signed only the parts she'd agreed to.\n\nThat was the kind of welcome worth keeping.","word_count":2192},"maui-47":{"number":47,"title":"Night at the Reopened Channel","content":"The rain arrived just after we finished fastening the last lantern hook.\n\nAt first it was only a mist, the kind that blurred the sea-glass orchard into soft green shapes and made every stone look freshly washed. Then the wind shifted, and real rain came across Tideglass in silver lines.\n\nFen looked at the gauges.\n\n\"Well,\" they said, \"here's our test.\"\n\nThe temporary surface channel had been cleaned, braced, and reopened after its controlled-flow trials. It carried water along an accessible maintenance route toward the shore, away from the gardens and lower homes. The older underground passage remained sealed. Nobody had pretended that building one good channel meant we could ignore the other forever.\n\nTonight's storm would tell us whether the surface repairs could hold through actual weather.\n\nTamsin stood by the upstream marker with a waterproof notebook. Danse monitored the portable pump. Sela watched the garden beds from beneath a canvas awning, refusing to leave until she knew her crops weren't about to drown again. Edda and Pip were checking the safe path, making sure the lanterns stayed visible through the rain.\n\nMaui carried a coil of spare rope.\n\nI carried hot tea.\n\nIt wasn't a glamorous division of labor.\n\nIt was ours.\n\nThe first hour passed slowly. Water rose against the channel's inner wall, then moved through the new outlet. The gauges stayed within the posted range. The lower service passage did not ring; Fen marked the absence of a sound as carefully as they would have marked its return.\n\nA burst of rain came harder.\n\nThe upstream reading climbed.\n\n\"Hold the flow,\" Fen called.\n\nDanse adjusted the pump. Tamsin checked the joint where the previous test had shown a leak. Sela leaned forward, watching the shallow runnel beside her garden.\n\nFor a moment the water edged toward the soil.\n\nMy heart lurched.\n\nThen the channel took the load and carried it away.\n\nSela released a breath she'd been holding for far too long.\n\n\"Good,\" she whispered.\n\nMaui looked at me.\n\nI could see the impulse to cheer, to lift someone into the air, to turn the moment into a victory for a hero. He stayed where he was and handed Fen the next measurement sheet.\n\nBy midnight the rain had eased.\n\nThe channel held through the tested conditions. The engineers would still need to inspect it in daylight, and the lower passage remained closed pending another round of structural work. The result was good, but it was not a guarantee for every storm the valley would ever see.\n\nWe recorded it honestly.\n\nThen we went home.\n\nMaui took off his wet cloak by the door. Water ran from the hem onto the floor. He looked at the puddle, then at me.\n\nMini Maui appeared at the edge of his shoulder and pantomimed a magnificent victory parade through our tiny kitchen.\n\n\"No,\" I said, before the real Maui had opened his mouth.\n\n\"How do you keep doing that?\"\n\n\"Your tattoos are terrible at keeping secrets.\"\n\nHe looked down. \"Traitor.\"\n\nThen he shook the cloak, sending water over both our feet, and bowed as if he'd just saved an entire fleet.\n\n\"Behold the mighty Maui, conqueror of the storm! Successfully carried rope, followed the map, and didn't collapse a single historic building. You're welcome.\"\n\n\"You're dripping on the clean floor.\"\n\n\"An outrageous criticism of a flawless hero.\"\n\nHis grin was bright enough to make me laugh, but when he looked toward the window I could see the worry he'd been hiding. The channel had held for tonight's conditions. Tomorrow might be different. He wanted an ending with a great speech. We had a wet kitchen and another inspection scheduled.\n\nHe fetched a cloth and wiped the floor while humming a terrible victory tune. It sounded suspiciously like a song he intended to perform to an audience of two.\n\n\"I could make a speech about what we survived,\" he said.\n\n\"Please don't.\"\n\n\"Not even a short one?\"\n\n\"You're dripping on the clean boards.\"\n\nHe laughed and fetched a cloth.\n\nBefore we went inside, Fen called us back to the tideboard. The gauge was below its warning line, but the rain had left a pile of leaves against the screen. The system had worked within the conditions posted for it; it wasn't a promise that tomorrow's debris would clear itself.\n\n\"We'll need a morning shift to inspect the grate,\" Fen said. \"No one goes down the historic barrier. That stays closed.\"\n\nMaui looked at the water, then at the coil of rope he'd carried all evening.\n\n\"I could stay and guard it.\"\n\n\"You could,\" Fen answered. \"Or you could let the paid watch do the job they signed up for.\"\n\nHe looked faintly wounded. \"Am I being dismissed?\"\n\n\"You're being thanked. Go home.\"\n\nTamsin sent the initial numbers to Neri for the public board, carefully separating observed flow from the forecast. Sela wanted to know whether the shallowest bed had survived. Fen told her what the gauge could establish and what she'd need to check in daylight. Nobody offered her a promise based on darkness and optimism.\n\nEdda came down the upper path after confirming the shelter door was visible and unlocked. She was tired enough to stop beside us and rub her wrists.\n\n\"Please don't ask me to plan another grand opening,\" she said. \"I have had enough ceremonies for a season.\"\n\n\"Not even a small one?\" Maui asked.\n\n\"Especially not a small one. Somebody always asks me to move the chairs.\"\n\nI laughed. We helped return the lanterns to their hooks, then signed out of the shift so Neri would know who remained on duty. The little act made the night feel less like an adventure and more like a place where people had learned to care for their own.\n\nAs we walked home, Maui reached for my hand, then withdrew it.\n\n\"Sorry. Your fingers are cold.\"\n\n\"They're cold because I was holding a mug in the rain. You can still ask.\"\n\nHe smiled. \"May I?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nHe took my hand between both of his, warming it as we passed the garden. For a moment I wanted to tell him that nothing else in the world mattered. That would have been romantic, and untrue. Sela's seedlings mattered. The paid watch mattered. Our friends and other partners mattered. I was glad there was room for all of it, even on a night when I wanted to go home with him more than anything.\n\nThe house lights came into view. He lifted our joined hands and kissed my knuckles, then opened the door for me. There was wet flooring to mop and tea to make, and for once we were both delighted to be ordinary.\n\nI lit the kitchen stove while he dried the floor. The house smelled of damp wool, warm wood, and the rosemary we'd hung to dry beside the window. Outside, the sea still moved against the stones, but the rain had softened to a steady whisper.\n\nWe made tea.\n\nNeither of us wanted dinner.\n\nFor a while we sat without speaking.\n\nMaui looked toward the window.\n\n\"Look at that,\" Maui said, gesturing toward our reflections in the rainy window. \"Two magnificent heroes. One with much better hair.\" His grin stayed in place for a moment, then slipped. \"Are you happy?\"\n\nI considered.\n\n\"I'm relieved. Tired. A little frightened about the next storm. And yes, I'm happy.\"\n\nHe smiled.\n\n\"I was thinking about the first time we stood by that landing.\"\n\n\"When you promised to fix everything?\"\n\n\"I was magnificent.\"\n\n\"You were unbearable.\"\n\n\"Both.\"\n\nI laughed.\n\nHe took my hand across the table.\n\n\"I thought if I could keep you safe, you'd never need to leave.\"\n\nI looked at him.\n\n\"And now?\"\n\n\"Now I know you have to be able to leave. Otherwise staying doesn't mean what I want it to mean.\"\n\nI felt my throat tighten.\n\n\"That's the thing I've been trying to learn too.\"\n\nHe moved closer.\n\n\"Can I ask for something?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Stay with me tonight. Not because the storm was frightening, or because the village needs us, or because I earned it. Because you want to.\"\n\nI looked at him.\n\n\"I do.\"\n\nHe leaned toward me slowly, giving me time to meet him. I kissed him, tasting the tea and the faint salt of the air. His fingers brushed my cheek, then settled against my waist when I drew him closer.\n\nFor a moment I forgot the soaked cloak, the gauges, the repairs. There was only the warmth of him, the small crackle of the fire, and the way his breath caught when I kissed him again.\n\n\"Are you cold?\" he whispered.\n\n\"A little.\"\n\n\"Would you like the blanket?\"\n\n\"Eventually.\"\n\nHe smiled against my mouth.\n\nI laughed.\n\nWe turned down the lamps and left the dishes for morning. The rest of the night belonged to us, quiet and tender behind a closed door, with no need to describe it to anyone else.\n\nWhen I woke, the rain had stopped.\n\nMaui was sitting on the edge of the bed, trying to untangle a knot in his hair with the kind of concentration he usually reserved for storms.\n\n\"Need help?\" I asked.\n\n\"Absolutely not.\"\n\nI watched him struggle for another minute.\n\n\"Would you like help?\" I asked again.\n\nHe sighed.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nI sat beside him, worked the knot free, and kissed his shoulder.\n\nOutside, Fen's dawn bell sounded once.\n\nThe old channel did not answer.\n\nBefore breakfast Fen stopped at the door carrying a damp notebook and the narrow pole used to measure debris at the upper grate. Their coat was still dripping from the night shift.\n\n“Don't celebrate yet,” they told Maui. “We need the first report written before anyone decides we can leave the barriers open indefinitely.”\n\nMaui had been halfway through lifting the breakfast table to move it out of their way. He set it down so gently that not even the cups rattled.\n\n“Aw, come on. The channel held!”\n\n“It did,” Fen said. “At the tide level we measured. Not every tide forever.”\n\nI took the notebook. The night crew had recorded three readings, one blocked branch and a short stretch of accessible path that would need new grip stones before the next storm. The lower historic passage remained sealed, and no one had used it to reach the inspection point.\n\n“Can we help with the path?” I asked.\n\n“Later, after the engineers approve the materials. Not by rearranging the shore before breakfast.”\n\nMaui pulled a face. Mini Maui threw up its tiny hands in outrage.\n\n“We are being persecuted by paperwork,” Maui announced.\n\n“By measuring things,” Fen corrected.\n\n“Even worse.”\n\nI laughed and offered Fen a dry cloth for the notebook. They accepted, then asked if the morning report could state the tide limit in plain language. Not every traveler would know what the marks on the gauge meant. The public notice needed to say when the upper route closed and whom to contact if the grate filled again.\n\nMaui found a pencil and added the word *ask* to the bottom of the draft. It was crooked. He looked very pleased with it.\n\n\nAt breakfast we received the inspection message: the temporary surface system had passed its first real storm test. Another inspection was scheduled, and the lower passage would remain sealed.\n\nMaui read the note aloud, then set it beside the calendar.\n\n\"Good enough for today,\" he said.\n\nI smiled.\n\nWe ate bread, cleaned the wet floor, and went to help Sela check the garden.\n\nNothing was finished forever.\n\nBut something important had held.","word_count":1924}}};for(const s of lib.series){const entries=changes[s.series_slug]||{};for(const ch of s.chapters){const e=entries[ch.id];if(e&&e.number===ch.chapter_number){ch.title=e.title;ch.content=e.content;ch.word_count=e.word_count;}}s.total_word_count=s.chapters.reduce((sum,ch)=>sum+(Number(ch.word_count)||0),0);}})();
